    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "68dcf9ee_b39ce592",
        "filename": "tools/testrunners/unit/BazelUnitTestRunner.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 18
      },
      "lineNbr": 46,
      "author": {
        "id": 10009
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-27T21:21:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Say we have a CI task that runs a non-Android GM target on a Skolo NUC of some kind. The Bazel invocation would be something like:\n\n```\n$ bazel test //gm:some-gm --config\u003dlinux_rbe --config\u003dsome-nuc\n```\n\nThe `some-nuc` config would be defined in `//bazel/devicesrc`. This file would include the following lines:\n\n```\n...\ntest:some-nuc --test_arg\u003d--device-specific-bazel-config\ntest:some-nuc --test_arg\u003dsome-nuc\n...\n```\n\nIn other words, Bazel would invoke the C++ binary with `--device-specific-bazel-config some-nuc`. Given how `CommandLineFlags.h` works, the C++ binary would fail with an \"unknown flag\" error, unless we define flag `--device-specific-bazel-config` explicitly. Then, we can safely ignore that flag.\n\nDoes that make sense?",
      "parentUuid": "ba433f71_4ce43909",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 46,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 46,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "b6896cb52de7b54d33c207ea5038beae34cc5e3d",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
